/* book Worked example: Q: A binary distillation column separates a mixture with constant relative volatility α = 2.5. Feed is saturated liquid with xF = 0.40, distillate xD = 0.95 and bottoms xB = 0.05 (all mole fractions of the more volatile component). Reflux ratio R = 2.0. (a) Write the rectifying operating line. (b) Find the feed intersection. (c) Write the stripping operating line. (d) Estimate the theoretical stages including the reboiler.
   1. Rectifying slope: R/(R+1) = 2/(2+1) = 0.6667. Intercept: xD/(R+1) = 0.95/3 = 0.3167. Rectifying line: y = 0.6667x + 0.3167.
   2. Saturated liquid feed means q = 1, so the q-line is vertical at xF = 0.40. At this x the rectifying line gives yF = 0.6667(0.40) + 0.3167 = 0.5833. Feed point P = (0.40, 0.5833).
   3. Equilibrium vapour at xF is yF* = 2.5(0.40)/[1 + 1.5(0.40)] = 1.00/1.60 = 0.6250. P lies below the equilibrium curve, as it must.
   4. Stripping line through (0.05, 0.05) and P: slope = (0.5833 − 0.05)/(0.40 − 0.05) = 0.5333/0.35 = 1.5238. Intercept = 0.05 − 1.5238(0.05) = −0.0262. Stripping line: y = 1.5238x − 0.0262.
   5. Step from (0.95, 0.95). First horizontal: y = 0.95 gives x* = 0.95/[2.5 − 1.5(0.95)] = 0.8837. First vertical to the rectifying line: y = 0.6667(0.8837) + 0.3167 = 0.9058. The table continues the staircase.
   6. Stage 7 is the first whose liquid (x = 0.3693) falls below xF = 0.40, so it straddles the q-line: it is the optimum feed stage, and from there on the vertical moves use the stripping line.
   7. Stage 13 gives x = 0.0322 < xB = 0.05, so the staircase ends there. Stage 13 is the partial reboiler; the column holds 12 ideal trays. The last step is about half used ((0.0675 − 0.05)/(0.0675 − 0.0322) = 0.50), so the exact count is about 12.5.
   Answer:  */
