/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - McCabe-Thiele assumes that each mole of vapour condensed sends one mole of liquid down, which needs equal latent heats and no heat losses.
   - The diagram has two enthalpy curves.
   - A difference point replaces the operating line.
   - Take an envelope round the condenser and the top of the column.
   - HΔD is the ordinate of the rectifying difference point (kJ/mol); H₁ is the enthalpy of the saturated vapour leaving the top tray at y₁ = xD (kJ/mol); hD is the enthalpy of the saturated reflux and distillate liquid (kJ/mol); QC is the conde
   - ΔD = (xD, HΔD) is a construction point, not a tray.
   - The lever rule on each operating line gives the internal reflux ratio: Ln/Vn+1 = (HΔD − Hn+1)/(HΔD − hn).
   - The stripping section has its own difference point ΔB at xB, with ordinate HΔB = hB − QB/B, where QB is the reboiler duty and B the bottoms flow.
   - McCabe-Thiele is the special case of horizontal curves, H = HV and h = hL with constant gap λ = HV − hL.
   - y is the vapour mole fraction on the McCabe-Thiele operating line; x is the liquid mole fraction; R is the reflux ratio; xD is the distillate mole fraction.
*/
