/* book Worked example: Q: Take H = 50 − 10y and h = 20 − 4x kJ/mol, so the latent heat falls from 30 to 24 kJ/mol. With α = 2.5, xD = 0.95, R = 2.0 (total condenser), saturated-liquid feed at zF = 0.40 and xB = 0.05, place ΔD and ΔB, step three trays from the top, and compare L/V with McCabe-Thiele.
   1. Top values: y₁ = 0.95, H₁ = 50 − 10(0.95) = 40.5 kJ/mol; hD = 20 − 4(0.95) = 16.2 kJ/mol.
   2. HΔD = 40.5 + 2.0(40.5 − 16.2) = 40.5 + 48.6 = 89.1 kJ/mol, so ΔD = (0.95, 89.1). Condenser duty per mole of distillate: 89.1 − 16.2 = 72.9 kJ/mol.
   3. Tray 1: tie line from y₁ = 0.95 gives x₁ = 0.95/(2.5 − 1.5 × 0.95) = 0.8837 and h₁ = 20 − 4(0.8837) = 16.47 kJ/mol.
   4. Line from ΔD through (0.8837, 16.47) has slope (89.1 − 16.47)/(0.95 − 0.8837) = 1096 kJ/mol per unit x. It cuts H = 50 − 10y at y₂ = 0.9061 (H₂ = 40.94). McCabe-Thiele gives 0.9058.
   5. Repeat for trays 2 and 3 (table): y₃ = 0.8474 and y₄ = 0.7800, against 0.8461 and 0.7764 from the McCabe-Thiele line at the same x.
   6. Internal reflux by the lever rule: L₁/V₂ = (89.1 − 40.94)/(89.1 − 16.47) = 0.663, then 0.658 and 0.653, all below R/(R + 1) = 0.667 and falling.
   7. ΔB: the feed point is (0.40, hF = 18.4). The line from ΔD through F has slope (89.1 − 18.4)/(0.95 − 0.40) = 128.5; at x = 0.05 it reaches 18.4 − 128.5 × 0.35 = −26.6 kJ/mol. So ΔB = (0.05, −26.6) and the reboiler supplies hB − HΔB = 19.8 + 26.6 = 46.4 kJ per mole of bottoms.
   Answer:  */
